# ---------------------------------------------------------------- S6 CRT
S6 = sh(
    "S6",
    r"The map $\Phi:x\mapsto\prod_ix_i^{\mu_i}$ is an algebra isomorphism from $\mathbb C[x]/(x^S-1)$ to $\mathbb C[x_1,\dots,x_d]/(x_i^{s_i}-1)$, so one cyclic convolution of length $S=s_1\cdots s_d$ becomes a $d$-dimensional cyclic convolution on pairwise coprime axes, implemented by triangular controlled shifts in time $O(Tp\,d^2(1+\ell^\tau))$.",
    "Define $\\Phi$ by the Chinese-remainder exponents $\\mu_i=P_i^{-1}\\bmod s_i$, check it is an isomorphism preserving convolution, and observe that in mixed-radix coordinates each new coordinate is a lower-triangular function of the old ones, hence a prefix-controlled cyclic shift.",
    mot("The transform lengths needed for the integer product are pairwise coprime, and a cyclic convolution of coprime length is a multidimensional cyclic convolution by the Chinese remainder theorem (Agarwal and Cooley).", "explicit",
        "The transform lengths needed for the integer product are pairwise coprime,"),
    [
        inf("S6.1",
            r"With pairwise coprime $s_1,\dots,s_d$, $S=\prod s_i$, put $P_i=\prod_{j<i}s_j$ and $\mu_i=P_i^{-1}\pmod{s_i}$.",
            r"P_i=\prod_{j<i}s_j,\qquad \mu_i=P_i^{-1}\pmod{s_i}."),
        inf("S6.2",
            r"The assignment $x\mapsto\prod_ix_i^{\mu_i}$ respects $x^S=1$, so it defines an algebra map $\Phi$; the image of $x$ has order $S$ because each $\mu_i$ is a unit and the $s_i$ are pairwise coprime, so its powers run through all target monomials exactly once and $\Phi$ is an isomorphism preserving cyclic convolution.",
            r"$\Phi$ is an isomorphism and preserves cyclic convolution."),
        inf("S6.3",
            r"Every coefficient index has a unique mixed-radix expression $k=\sum_{j=1}^da_jP_j$ with $0\le a_j<s_j$, and the coordinate of $\Phi(x^k)$ in axis $i$ is $b_i=a_i+\mu_i\sum_{j<i}a_jP_j\pmod{s_i}$, since terms with $j>i$ are divisible by $s_i$ and $\mu_iP_i=1$ modulo $s_i$.",
            r"b_i=a_i+\mu_i\sum_{j<i}a_jP_j\pmod{s_i}."),
        inf("S6.4",
            r"Numerical order of $k$ is lexicographic order of $(a_d,\dots,a_1)$, so padding is inserted by one nested scan of the full box $\prod_i[0,t_i)$: at an address with every $a_i<s_i$ copy the next coefficient record, elsewhere write a zero record; the valid addresses occur in the original order, and deletion uses the same enumeration, each in $O(Tp)$.",
            r"Insert the padding in one nested scan of the full"),
        inf("S6.5",
            r"After reordering the padded axes to $(a_1,\dots,a_d)$, update the targets in the order $i=d,d-1,\dots,1$, so that when target $i$ is updated none of its lower-index controls has changed yet.",
            r"order $i=d,d-1,\ldots,1$ using \eqref{eq:triangular-crt}."),
        inf("S6.6",
            r"On a fixed control prefix rotate the valid target interval $[0,s_i)$ by the offset and fix $[s_i,t_i)$, using the identity if a lower-index control is invalid; this is a bijection of the padded box preserving the valid set and its complement, so zero padding remains zero after every step, and an explicit inverse runs $i=1,\dots,d$.",
            r"permutation of the valid target interval and the identity elsewhere."),
        inf("S6.7",
            r"Each update is a prefix-controlled cyclic shift: prepare the offset, split the valid target interval into its two consecutive pieces, merge them in the opposite order; there are at most $T/t_i$ control prefixes for target $i$, so all offset preparation costs $O(dTp^C/r)=o(Tp)$.",
            r"offsets therefore cost $O(dTp^C/r)=o(Tp)$ to prepare, since"),
        inf("S6.8",
            r"Axis widths are $\ell$ or $\ell-1$; two adjacent axes of unequal width are exchanged by moving the longer one's leading bit out, swapping the equal-width pair with the chunk interchange, and putting the bit back; reversing all axes uses $O(d^2)$ such moves, so $\Phi$, its inverse and their padding operations cost $O(Tp\,d^2(1+\ell^\tau))$.",
            r"O\bigl(Tp\,d^2(1+\ell^\tau)\bigr)."),
    ],
    page="crt-cyclic-convolution",
    frm=["controlled-shifts", "chunk-interchange", "modular-arithmetic-and-congruences", "primes-coprimality-and-gcd", "euclidean-algorithm-and-modular-inverses", "cartesian-products-and-lexicographic-order"],
    adds=[
        ad("P_i", "prefix product", "The integer product $\\prod_{j<i}s_j$ of the source lengths of the axes before axis $i$."),
        ad("\\mu_i", "CRT exponent", "The inverse of $P_i$ modulo $s_i$, used as exponent of $x_i$ in the image of $x$."),
        ad("\\Phi", "CRT map", "The algebra isomorphism from $\\mathbb C[x]/(x^S-1)$ to the product of cyclic group algebras sending $x$ to $\\prod_ix_i^{\\mu_i}$."),
    ],
)

